well joining me now is stephen kinnick  the shadow minister for immigration good  to talk to you this morning uh mr clinic  do you think ukraine can win this on its  own  i think that president putin has made a  massive strategic miscalculation  you cannot with an army of 150 000 hope  to occupy an entire country of 44  million people where everybody hates you  and at the most they may try to hold  kievan kharkiv but the rest of the  country will resist they will fight the  resistance the courage the determination  of the ukrainian people is an  inspiration to us all and mr putin has  clearly bitten off more than he can chew  and the key now will be to resist uh  push him back  and i am absolutely convinced that the  russians will be defeated in this war  they will be expelled from ukraine and  ukraine will return to being a free  democratic and sovereign country  but at what cost and how long would that  potentially take  well uh what we know in russia is that  there are people around vladimir putin  of course who are deeply corrupt  and very bad people but they also have a  very strong desire for self-preservation  and at some point they will realize that  their self-preservation depends more on  getting rid of mr putin than on him  continuing and as uh more and more  russian soldiers are dying in the field  as there is less and less understanding  in russia as to why they are waging this  war mr putin's position will become  increasingly  shaky what we will also see is  hugely increased isolation in terms of  the russian economy that really is  already beginning to bite we're seeing  massive inflation  and weakening of the ruble  and a huge increase in interest rates  this is all starting to affect the  oligarchs and the kleptocrats that prop  mr putin up and that's the key is to  make the russian elite realize military  and security and political elites  realize uh that they are backing the  wrong horse here  and that that has to change and that i  think is going to be very important for  the west to stand together very strongly  in that we've been too naive and too  complacent in the west for too long the  conservative governments in this country  since 2010 have rolled out the red  carpet for authoritarian regimes uh like  china they've allowed russia they've  allowed london to become  awash with  dirty russian money  and we need to see all of that changing  now we need to see a reform of companies  house we need to see a proper  overseas  entities bill so that we can see who  actually owns these assets we need to  see the sanctions that are being carried  out against russian oligarchs be working  far more quickly uh the asset  freezes are too slow and there's a real  risk of asset flight as a lot of these  oligarchs will just start to sell up all  their assets  and that will not be achieving the  objectives that we've set out so  you know we're moving in the right  direction but it's been too slow this is  a huge reality check i think for our  country and for the whole of europe  we've got to rise to this challenge  because our values democracy and freedom  are on the line  do you think those sanctions should  extend to roman abramovich  yes absolutely uh there is a list very  clearly  list of of names that's been published  for example  by alexey navalny the opposition leader  uh in uh in russia it includes people  like abramovic  like usmanov  we've seen the european union and  the united states publishing those names  i am just mystified as to why the  british government is not publishing the  names of the people that we are  sanctioning and getting on with the job  it's too slow uh it's it just looks like  foot dragging there's arguments about  the legal side of it but the  government's got to step up and show the  leadership that we must show  just to reflect the tremendous courage  and leadership that the ukrainian people  are showing it is the very least that we  can do for them  and we've got to move forward  more quickly those asset  freezes need to be put in place very  very quickly  and this is personal for you isn't it  you used to live in russia you worked in  saint petersburg with the british ancel  you were arrested while you were out  there how do you personally view what  what putin is doing  well  my memory from russia is just the  enormous gap that there is between the  russian people and their leadership the  russian people want to live normal lives  they want to be a normal country that is  connected to the rest of the world there  are a strong and resilient people but  they're so badly led they are led by  deeply corrupt thugs and gangsters who  are  letting russia down and are only  interested in holding on to power and  lining their own pockets  so it always made me very sad when i was  in russia to see that and and of course  we in the end in the british council we  we stood up to the russians uh they they  sought to close down our our operations  and and we stood up to mr putin because  we knew that uh the the values that we  hold dear and our desire  for  building better relationships between  the british people and the russian  people that was more important than  dancing to mr putin's tune  and that i think is what we have to take  away from this is that the bullies only  understand the language of strength and  unity and consistency  and the moment that they  smell weakness they will exploit that  and they will go for you so you have to  be ready to to push back we need to  build our resilience uh as a country  against these authoritarian regimes that  are seeking to undermine our democracy  because you can't go out and project  strength in the world until you get your  own house in order uh back at home so  we've got to learn those lessons  russia and the russian people  it's a great country and a great people  but  these corrupt  dictators  are badly letting them down we have to  stand up against that  and we have to do it in a way that is  united and strong with our  democratic allies and partners
 

